    ]
  },
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "adr-marketplace-payments-model-1",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "ADR: Facilitated split payments, platform never holds funds",
      "width": 180,
      "height": 52,
      "collapsed": false,
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "status": "proposed",
      "date": "",
      "context": "A two-sided marketplace moves money it doesn't ultimately own, from buyers to sellers, minus commission. How that money is held in transit determines whether the platform takes on money-transmitter obligations, who bears chargeback risk, and how hard it is to guarantee sellers actually get paid.",
      "decision": "Use a managed payments-facilitator provider's split-payment product: the provider holds funds in transit and executes the split — seller share, platform commission — at payout time. The platform never custodies buyer funds directly; it only instructs the provider what split to apply and when to release it.",
      "consequences": "• The platform operates as a facilitator under the provider's money-transmitter licence rather than needing its own, removing years of regulatory work from the critical path to launch.\n• Commission collection is guaranteed by construction — deducted before the seller is paid, not invoiced separately and hoped for.\n• The platform's control over payout timing is limited to what the provider's API exposes; a provider incident or policy change becomes a dependency the marketplace can't fully route around.\n• Refunds and chargebacks flow through the provider's split-payment reversal logic, which the order and dispute workflow must model explicitly rather than treating payment as one atomic step.",
      "alternatives": "1. Platform as merchant of record, invoicing sellers for commission separately — the platform bears full chargeback and fraud liability on every transaction and likely crosses into money-transmitter territory on its own.\n2. Buyer pays seller directly, platform only invoices commission after the fact — simplest to build and clearly outside payments regulation, but the platform has no mechanism to guarantee it collects its commission or to protect buyers with a dispute process.\n3. Build a full in-house ledger and banking-rail integration — maximum control and no per-transaction provider fee, but a multi-year regulatory and engineering undertaking before the first transaction can move.\n4. Managed split-payment facilitator (chosen) — fastest path to a compliant, chargeback-protected marketplace, at the cost of depending on the provider's product and pricing."
    }
  ]
}
